  "title": "The Exchange and its idea",
  "intro": "The Exchange Ruy Lopez gives up a bishop for a knight on move four and asks one question for the rest of the game: whose pawn majority is worth more.\n\n## The trade, and what it buys\n\nAfter 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5 a6 4.Bxc6 dxc6 Black has the two bishops, which are worth something real in an open position. What White has is a structure that does not change for the rest of the game. Once the centre pawns come off, White has four pawns against three on the kingside and every one of them healthy. Black has four against three on the queenside and two of them stand on the c-file.\n\nOnly one of those majorities can make a passed pawn. Push Black's queenside and the c-pawns stay in each other's way; the a-pawn cannot outrun two white pawns on its own. Push White's kingside and a passer appears.\n\nSo White plays towards an endgame and Black plays to avoid one. That single sentence explains most of the moves in this course.\n\n## Your plan, in four steps\n\nCastle. Play d4 to make Black's centre pawn commit, and recapture on d4 with a piece. Take every queen trade you are offered, because each one is a step towards the ending the structure decides. Then advance the kingside majority with f4 and e5, or bring a knight to c4 and f5 while Black's bishops have nothing to bite on.\n\n## The one thing never to do\n\nDo not recapture on f3 with the g-pawn. Black offers that trade in three different chapters, and every time it doubles pawns on the very wing this whole opening exists to exploit. Recapture with the queen.\n\n## What Black is up to\n\nKeep pieces on, open lines for the bishops, and hit e4 and f3 with ...f6, ...Bg4 and ...Qf6. Black is not worse here. This is a fight and not a technique, and it is a fight where one wrong trade by Black leads to a lost ending.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTaking on e5 with the knight before the position allows it. Recapturing towards the centre with the g-pawn and wrecking the majority. Playing d4 before castling. And trading pieces cheerfully because trades are usually good: here they are good because of the structure, and against the two bishops in an open position they are not good at all.",
